00:04.196 --> 00:07.656 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 1

00:07.656 --> 00:09.616 align:middle
of 25 Transcript of Chair
Powell's Press Conference March

00:09.616 --> 00:11.136 align:middle
18, 2026 CHAIR POWELL.

00:11.136 --> 00:11.846 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:11.846 --> 00:14.176 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:14.176 --> 00:17.406 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:17.406 --> 00:20.046 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:21.456 --> 00:24.256 align:middle
The U.S. economy has been
expanding at a solid pace.

00:25.086 --> 00:26.786 align:middle
While job gains have
remained low,

00:26.946 --> 00:29.646 align:middle
the unemployment rate has been
little changed in recent months,

00:30.256 --> 00:32.236 align:middle
and inflation remains
somewhat elevated.

00:33.606 --> 00:37.266 align:middle
Today, the FOMC decided to
leave our policy rate unchanged.

00:38.336 --> 00:41.406 align:middle
We see the current stance of
monetary policy as appropriate

00:41.826 --> 00:44.126 align:middle
to promote progress toward
our maximum-employment

00:44.556 --> 00:46.366 align:middle
and 2 percent inflation goals.

00:46.856 --> 00:50.496 align:middle
The implications of
developments in the Middle East

00:50.496 --> 00:52.416 align:middle
for the U.S. economy
are uncertain.

00:53.736 --> 00:55.686 align:middle
We will remain attentive
to risks

00:55.686 --> 00:57.346 align:middle
to both sides of
our dual mandate.

00:58.326 --> 01:00.436 align:middle
And I'll have more to
say about monetary policy

01:00.436 --> 01:03.066 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:05.056 --> 01:06.426 align:middle
Available indicators suggest

01:06.426 --> 01:09.536 align:middle
that economic activity has
been expanding at a solid pace.

01:10.436 --> 01:12.066 align:middle
Consumer spending
has been resilient,

01:12.716 --> 01:15.246 align:middle
and business fixed investment
has continued to expand.

01:16.456 --> 01:17.656 align:middle
In contrast, activity

01:17.656 --> 01:19.536 align:middle
in the housing sector
has remained weak.

01:21.036 --> 01:22.756 align:middle
In our Summary of
Economic Projections,

01:22.786 --> 01:24.606 align:middle
the median participant projects

01:24.606 --> 01:27.486 align:middle
that real GDP will rise
2.4 percent this year

01:28.046 --> 01:31.126 align:middle
and 2.3 percent next
year, somewhat stronger

01:31.126 --> 01:32.516 align:middle
than projected in December.

01:34.306 --> 01:35.086 align:middle
In the labor market,

01:35.256 --> 01:38.226 align:middle
the unemployment rate was
4.4 percent in February

01:38.406 --> 01:41.346 align:middle
and has changed little
since late last summer.

01:42.866 --> 01:44.346 align:middle
Job gains have remained low.

01:45.526 --> 01:48.026 align:middle
A good part of the slowing
in the pace of job growth

01:48.026 --> 01:51.096 align:middle
over the past year reflects
a decline in the growth

01:51.136 --> 01:54.036 align:middle
of the labor force due
to lower immigration

01:54.036 --> 01:55.486 align:middle
and labor force participation,

01:56.406 --> 01:58.796 align:middle
though labor demand has
clearly softened as well.

02:00.526 --> 02:04.286 align:middle
Other indicators, including
job openings, layoffs, hiring,

02:04.286 --> 02:05.546 align:middle
and nominal wage growth,

02:06.006 --> 02:08.366 align:middle
generally show little
change in recent months.

02:09.926 --> 02:11.986 align:middle
In our SEP, the median
projection

02:11.986 --> 02:15.516 align:middle
of the unemployment rate is 4.4
percent at the end of this year

02:15.516 --> 02:16.136 align:middle
and edges down thereafter.

02:16.136 --> 02:19.256 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 2

02:19.256 --> 02:21.886 align:middle
of 25 Inflation has eased
significantly from its highs

02:21.886 --> 02:25.866 align:middle
in mid-2022 but remains
somewhat elevated relative

02:25.916 --> 02:27.676 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

02:28.826 --> 02:30.816 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index

02:30.816 --> 02:32.186 align:middle
and other data indicate

02:32.186 --> 02:35.656 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 2.8 percent

02:36.076 --> 02:38.606 align:middle
over the 12 months ending
in February and that,

02:38.606 --> 02:41.206 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

02:41.526 --> 02:44.766 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
3.0 percent.

02:45.676 --> 02:48.536 align:middle
These elevated readings
largely reflect inflation

02:48.536 --> 02:50.866 align:middle
in the goods sector,
which has been boosted

02:50.956 --> 02:52.416 align:middle
by the effects of tariffs.

02:54.246 --> 02:56.916 align:middle
Near-term measures of inflation
expectations have risen

02:56.916 --> 02:59.816 align:middle
in recent weeks, likely
reflecting the substantial rise

02:59.816 --> 03:01.296 align:middle
in oil prices caused

03:01.296 --> 03:03.386 align:middle
by the supply disruptions
in the Middle East.

03:04.656 --> 03:07.846 align:middle
Most measures of longer-term
expectations remain consistent

03:07.846 --> 03:09.546 align:middle
with our 2 percent
inflation goal.

03:11.536 --> 03:13.186 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

03:13.186 --> 03:17.506 align:middle
for total PCE inflation
this year is 2.7 percent

03:17.896 --> 03:20.716 align:middle
and 2.2 percent next
year, a bit higher

03:20.716 --> 03:21.856 align:middle
than projected in December.

03:24.126 --> 03:27.296 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

03:27.296 --> 03:29.836 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

03:29.836 --> 03:30.836 align:middle
for the American people.

03:31.526 --> 03:33.486 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

03:33.486 --> 03:35.766 align:middle
to maintain the target range
for the federal funds rate

03:36.186 --> 03:38.236 align:middle
at 3½ to 3  percent.

03:39.466 --> 03:41.006 align:middle
From last September
through December,

03:41.546 --> 03:44.416 align:middle
we lowered our policy
rate   percentage point,

03:45.006 --> 03:47.726 align:middle
bringing it within a range of
plausible estimates of neutral.

03:48.826 --> 03:52.016 align:middle
This normalization of our
policy stance should continue

03:52.016 --> 03:55.166 align:middle
to help stabilize the labor
market while allowing inflation

03:55.166 --> 03:57.776 align:middle
to resume its downward
trend toward 2 percent.

03:59.106 --> 04:01.306 align:middle
But the implications of
events in the Middle East

04:01.306 --> 04:03.046 align:middle
for the U.S. economy
are uncertain.

04:03.716 --> 04:06.026 align:middle
In the near term, higher
energy prices will push

04:06.026 --> 04:09.896 align:middle
up overall inflation, but it
is too soon to know the scope

04:09.896 --> 04:12.926 align:middle
and duration of the potential
effects on the economy.

04:14.146 --> 04:15.756 align:middle
We will continue to
monitor the risks

04:15.756 --> 04:17.186 align:middle
to both sides of our mandate.

04:17.966 --> 04:20.496 align:middle
We are well positioned to
determine the extent and timing

04:20.496 --> 04:23.516 align:middle
of additional adjustments
to our policy rate based

04:23.516 --> 04:25.936 align:middle
on the incoming data,
the evolving outlook,

04:26.486 --> 04:27.546 align:middle
and the balance of risks.

04:27.546 --> 04:30.716 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 3

04:30.716 --> 04:32.276 align:middle
of 25 In our SEP, FOMC
participants wrote

04:32.276 --> 04:33.806 align:middle
down their individual
assessments

04:33.806 --> 04:35.916 align:middle
of an appropriate path
of the federal funds rate

04:36.456 --> 04:38.186 align:middle
under what each participant
judges

04:38.186 --> 04:40.646 align:middle
to be the most likely
scenario for the economy.

04:42.326 --> 04:45.096 align:middle
The median participant projects
that the appropriate level

04:45.096 --> 04:47.946 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
will be 3.4 percent at the end

04:47.946 --> 04:51.156 align:middle
of this year and 3.1 percent
at the end of next year,

04:51.656 --> 04:52.816 align:middle
unchanged from December.

04:54.056 --> 04:56.856 align:middle
As is always the case, these
individual forecasts are subject

04:56.856 --> 05:00.476 align:middle
to uncertainty, and they are not
a Committee plan or decision.

05:02.116 --> 05:04.356 align:middle
Monetary policy is not
on a preset course,

05:04.356 --> 05:07.166 align:middle
and we'll make our decisions
on a meeting-by-meeting basis.

05:08.776 --> 05:11.956 align:middle
To conclude: The Fed has
been assigned two goals

05:11.956 --> 05:15.206 align:middle
for monetary policy-maximum
employment and stable prices.

05:15.736 --> 05:18.346 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

05:18.896 --> 05:21.456 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

05:22.096 --> 05:24.506 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

05:24.626 --> 05:25.236 align:middle
well anchored.

05:26.506 --> 05:28.326 align:middle
Our success in delivering

05:28.326 --> 05:30.346 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

05:31.056 --> 05:34.276 align:middle
We at the Fed will continue to
do our jobs with objectivity,

05:34.696 --> 05:37.996 align:middle
integrity, and a deep commitment
to serve the American people.

05:39.106 --> 05:39.426 align:middle
Thank you.

05:39.626 --> 05:40.976 align:middle
And I look forward
to your questions.

05:41.736 --> 05:43.056 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

05:43.976 --> 05:44.986 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

05:45.126 --> 05:45.546 align:middle
Thank you.

05:45.546 --> 05:46.876 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the New York Times.

05:47.256 --> 05:49.566 align:middle
There's been some debate
about whether the Fed should

05:49.616 --> 05:51.186 align:middle
"look through" the
inflation that will come

05:51.186 --> 05:54.626 align:middle
from higher oil prices stemming
from the Middle East conflict.

05:54.626 --> 05:57.596 align:middle
Is that the right approach
at this juncture-and

05:57.596 --> 06:00.316 align:middle
to what extent does the
fact that inflation has been

06:00.316 --> 06:03.446 align:middle
above target for roughly
five years now influence the

06:03.446 --> 06:04.436 align:middle
Committee's thinking
around this?

06:04.856 --> 06:06.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

06:06.786 --> 06:12.586 align:middle
So first let me say, we're
well aware of the performance

06:12.586 --> 06:15.316 align:middle
of inflation over the last
few years and how a series

06:15.316 --> 06:17.616 align:middle
of shocks have interrupted
progress

06:17.616 --> 06:18.576 align:middle
that we've made over time.

06:19.236 --> 06:21.466 align:middle
And that happened most
recently with tariffs,

06:21.466 --> 06:23.866 align:middle
and then-and now there
will be some effects

06:23.866 --> 06:25.246 align:middle
on inflation coming forward.

06:25.246 --> 06:27.376 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 4

06:27.376 --> 06:29.106 align:middle
of 25 The, the thing
that's really important

06:29.106 --> 06:33.926 align:middle
that we see this year
is progress on inflation

06:34.326 --> 06:36.686 align:middle
through a reduction
in goods inflation

06:37.256 --> 06:40.916 align:middle
as the one-time effects
on prices of tariffs go

06:40.946 --> 06:42.466 align:middle
through the system-go
through the economy.

06:42.836 --> 06:44.426 align:middle
That's the main thing
we're looking for,

06:44.596 --> 06:45.916 align:middle
going into this exercise.

06:46.396 --> 06:49.856 align:middle
And we need to be
seeing that to, you know,

06:49.856 --> 06:52.486 align:middle
to sort of understand that we
actually are making progress

06:52.486 --> 06:54.526 align:middle
because, on net, we
didn't make progress.

06:54.866 --> 06:56.896 align:middle
And if you look at
total inflation-sorry,

06:57.016 --> 07:01.026 align:middle
total core inflation, it's about
3 percent, and some big chunk

07:01.026 --> 07:02.616 align:middle
of that, between a half
and three-quarters,

07:02.616 --> 07:04.996 align:middle
is actually tariffs, so we're
looking for progress on that.

07:05.976 --> 07:06.956 align:middle
The question of whether we

07:06.956 --> 07:11.006 align:middle
"look through" the energy
inflation doesn't really arise

07:11.006 --> 07:13.126 align:middle
until we have kind
of checked that box.

07:13.696 --> 07:16.386 align:middle
It, of course, is kind of
standard learning that you

07:16.386 --> 07:19.746 align:middle
"look through" energy shocks,
but that's always been dependent

07:19.846 --> 07:23.056 align:middle
on inflation expectations
remaining well anchored.

07:23.056 --> 07:24.906 align:middle
And I think now it's
also dependent now

07:24.906 --> 07:27.626 align:middle
on what you mentioned, which
is that broader context

07:28.276 --> 07:30.406 align:middle
of five years now of
inflation above target.

07:30.406 --> 07:33.596 align:middle
We have to keep all of those
things in mind, and the question

07:33.596 --> 07:35.156 align:middle
of "looking through,"
when it does arise,

07:35.156 --> 07:38.846 align:middle
will be one to approach
not lightly but, you know,

07:38.846 --> 07:40.486 align:middle
in the context that
you mentioned.

07:41.156 --> 07:41.476 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

07:41.476 --> 07:44.176 align:middle
And just on the SEP, can
you help us make sense

07:44.176 --> 07:46.086 align:middle
of why there is still
a bias to cut

07:46.216 --> 07:49.236 align:middle
for most officials this year,
despite the upward revision

07:49.236 --> 07:50.786 align:middle
to headline and core inflation,

07:51.006 --> 07:53.006 align:middle
and the essentially
unchanged forecast

07:53.006 --> 07:54.186 align:middle
for growth in unemployment?

07:54.456 --> 07:55.546 align:middle
Just curious kind of what,

07:56.236 --> 07:58.556 align:middle
what's the genesis behind
the need-what's the need

07:58.586 --> 07:59.136 align:middle
for the cut?

07:59.246 --> 07:59.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:59.776 --> 08:02.946 align:middle
Yeah, so there are 19
people and so 19 reasons,

08:02.946 --> 08:04.636 align:middle
19 individual submissions.

08:05.096 --> 08:07.276 align:middle
You know, but, but-and
if you notice,

08:07.276 --> 08:09.256 align:middle
the median didn't change,

08:09.656 --> 08:12.896 align:middle
but there was actually some
movement toward-a meaningful

08:12.896 --> 08:16.026 align:middle
amount of movement toward,
toward fewer cuts by people.

08:16.026 --> 08:18.926 align:middle
So four or five people
went from two to one,

08:18.926 --> 08:21.026 align:middle
let's say-two cuts to one cut.

08:21.026 --> 08:23.426 align:middle
And each person has
individual, you know,

08:23.426 --> 08:24.326 align:middle
stories behind what
they want to do.

08:24.326 --> 08:25.946 align:middle
But essentially it is March 18,

08:26.546 --> 08:30.166 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 5

08:30.166 --> 08:32.596 align:middle
of 25 that, you know,
the, the forecast is

08:33.156 --> 08:35.106 align:middle
that we'll be making
progress on inflation.

08:35.106 --> 08:37.716 align:middle
Not as much as we had hoped,
but some progress on inflation.

08:37.926 --> 08:40.266 align:middle
It should come as we
start to see in the middle

08:40.266 --> 08:44.516 align:middle
of the year progress on
tariffs going through once

08:44.516 --> 08:46.156 align:middle
and then tariff inflation
coming down.

08:46.156 --> 08:47.416 align:middle
That's-we should be seeing that.

08:48.006 --> 08:50.946 align:middle
And the rate forecast
is conditional

08:51.046 --> 08:52.506 align:middle
on the performance
of the economy.

08:52.506 --> 08:54.496 align:middle
So if we don't see
that progress,

08:54.536 --> 08:55.706 align:middle
then you won't see the rate cut.

08:57.036 --> 08:59.096 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:03.856 --> 09:04.576 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

09:04.576 --> 09:04.926 align:middle
Thank you.

09:04.926 --> 09:06.486 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

09:06.486 --> 09:09.396 align:middle
Just to follow up a little
bit, and to be clear:

09:09.396 --> 09:11.946 align:middle
Is the higher inflation
penciled in here

09:11.946 --> 09:16.476 align:middle
for 2026 solely the
result of the oil shock?

09:16.476 --> 09:17.816 align:middle
Or something else?

09:19.076 --> 09:19.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:20.016 --> 09:21.216 align:middle
So that's going to
be part of it.

09:21.546 --> 09:23.856 align:middle
But that wouldn't be
most of core, right?

09:23.856 --> 09:25.786 align:middle
So the oil shock for
sure shows up here.

09:26.466 --> 09:28.316 align:middle
Some of that will be in
core as well, but yes.

09:28.346 --> 09:31.246 align:middle
But, no, there's
also just the feeling

09:31.246 --> 09:33.956 align:middle
that we haven't seen the
progress that we had hoped

09:33.956 --> 09:38.956 align:middle
for on core goods and on
tariffs and on the rest of it.

09:38.956 --> 09:42.896 align:middle
You know, we've, we've-so for
whatever reason people did write

09:42.896 --> 09:46.026 align:middle
up their inflation forecast,
that will certainly be tied

09:46.076 --> 09:49.446 align:middle
to events in the Middle East and
the price of oil, but it's also,

09:49.446 --> 09:53.196 align:middle
I think, a reflection of
the slow progress we've seen

09:53.196 --> 09:55.426 align:middle
on tariffs-which we
believe we will see.

09:55.716 --> 09:58.316 align:middle
It's just a question of
how long it takes for them

09:58.316 --> 09:59.996 align:middle
to get all the way
through the economy.

09:59.996 --> 10:01.356 align:middle
And it takes-it just
takes some time.

10:01.496 --> 10:02.046 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

10:02.046 --> 10:05.146 align:middle
And is the-is the lack of
change in the SEPs-again,

10:05.146 --> 10:09.956 align:middle
just to explicate this a little
bit-more due to the expectation

10:09.956 --> 10:12.776 align:middle
that the oil portion of
this will pass through,

10:13.396 --> 10:17.656 align:middle
or more out of concern that
there is a potential blow

10:17.656 --> 10:20.916 align:middle
to consumption and growth coming
in the form of wealth effects

10:20.916 --> 10:22.166 align:middle
with the stock market down,

10:22.166 --> 10:23.696 align:middle
in the form of gas
prices diverting spending

10:23.696 --> 10:24.506 align:middle
from other parts of the economy?

10:24.666 --> 10:25.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:25.146 --> 10:27.686 align:middle
So I, I didn't get the
second part of your question.

10:27.686 --> 10:30.876 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 6

10:30.876 --> 10:32.016 align:middle
of 25 HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

10:32.016 --> 10:33.356 align:middle
Well, that consumers are going

10:33.356 --> 10:35.776 align:middle
to be potentially
diverting money to gasoline

10:36.286 --> 10:37.626 align:middle
from other parts of the economy.

10:37.626 --> 10:41.156 align:middle
There could be a growth shock or
redistribution of spending there

10:41.156 --> 10:42.166 align:middle
that can hit consumption.

10:42.596 --> 10:43.956 align:middle
Wealth effects as well.

10:43.956 --> 10:46.616 align:middle
I guess I'm wondering, are you
not-is the rate forecast not

10:46.616 --> 10:48.556 align:middle
changing because you think
the oil shock is going

10:48.556 --> 10:50.726 align:middle
to be temporary,
or because you want

10:50.726 --> 10:52.826 align:middle
to be sure you're positioned
in case growth starts to slow?

10:52.826 --> 10:53.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:53.136 --> 10:56.156 align:middle
So the thing I really want to
emphasize is that nobody knows.

10:56.246 --> 10:59.176 align:middle
The economics effect could be
bigger, they could be smaller,

10:59.176 --> 11:00.836 align:middle
they could be much
smaller or much bigger.

11:01.086 --> 11:02.066 align:middle
We just don't know.

11:02.066 --> 11:04.486 align:middle
So people are writing
down something that seems

11:04.486 --> 11:07.676 align:middle
to make sense to them but have
no conviction that's-to your

11:07.676 --> 11:12.096 align:middle
point, if we have high-if
we have a long period

11:12.096 --> 11:14.526 align:middle
of much higher gas prices,
that's going to weigh

11:14.526 --> 11:17.066 align:middle
on consumption, that will weigh
on disposable personal income,

11:17.616 --> 11:18.926 align:middle
and it'll weigh on consumption.

11:18.926 --> 11:20.396 align:middle
But we don't know
if that's going

11:20.396 --> 11:22.286 align:middle
to happen-something quite
different from that.

11:22.286 --> 11:24.016 align:middle
We might have much
lower-than-expected

11:24.346 --> 11:24.896 align:middle
pass-through.

11:24.946 --> 11:29.096 align:middle
So people write down-this is
one of those SEPs where a number

11:29.096 --> 11:32.136 align:middle
of people mentioned if we were
ever going to skip an SEP,

11:32.136 --> 11:35.276 align:middle
this would be a good one,
because we just don't know.

11:35.396 --> 11:39.466 align:middle
So I wouldn't say there's a
conviction that this is going

11:39.466 --> 11:41.866 align:middle
to go through quickly, or not
quickly, it just-you've got

11:41.866 --> 11:42.926 align:middle
to write something down.

11:43.396 --> 11:46.816 align:middle
And this is something that
is-that people wrote down.

11:47.426 --> 11:50.936 align:middle
And so-and we don't
debate how long.

11:50.936 --> 11:51.866 align:middle
How do you do that?

11:51.866 --> 11:54.666 align:middle
We wouldn't be able to debate
what the length or size

11:54.666 --> 11:56.366 align:middle
of these effects would
be-we just have to kind

11:56.366 --> 11:58.706 align:middle
of make an individual statement.

11:58.706 --> 12:00.486 align:middle
And also what you've written

12:00.486 --> 12:03.996 align:middle
down before will be-you'll be
reluctant to move too far away

12:03.996 --> 12:06.626 align:middle
from that, just because
you don't know-it's

12:06.626 --> 12:08.186 align:middle
so unclear what the direction

12:08.186 --> 12:10.046 align:middle
from this particular
end will be.

12:10.046 --> 12:12.836 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 7

12:12.836 --> 12:14.706 align:middle
of 25 Meanwhile, the economy
has-growth has been solid.

12:14.706 --> 12:16.306 align:middle
And inflation-the
overshoot is mainly

12:16.666 --> 12:18.346 align:middle
from the goods [sector]
and tariffs.

12:18.966 --> 12:23.706 align:middle
And the labor market is-the
unemployment rate, of course,

12:23.706 --> 12:25.246 align:middle
is little changed
since September.

12:25.386 --> 12:29.156 align:middle
You have a very, very low
breakeven rate, apparently,

12:29.156 --> 12:32.936 align:middle
for new jobs, with little growth
in either demand or supply.

12:32.976 --> 12:36.446 align:middle
But the U.S. economy
is doing pretty well.

12:36.946 --> 12:39.406 align:middle
It's just, we don't know what
the effects of this will be,

12:39.406 --> 12:40.446 align:middle
and really no one does.

12:40.876 --> 12:41.336 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:42.176 --> 12:45.656 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

12:45.686 --> 12:48.076 align:middle
Mr. Chair, in the past,
the staff has suggested

12:48.076 --> 12:52.956 align:middle
that higher oil prices-they
have a hit to consumption,

12:52.956 --> 12:55.766 align:middle
which Howard was talking about,
but that's somewhat offset

12:55.766 --> 12:58.166 align:middle
by increases to domestic
production.

12:58.516 --> 12:59.866 align:middle
I wonder if you could
talk about that dynamic,

12:59.866 --> 13:00.946 align:middle
especially to the extent

13:01.286 --> 13:03.926 align:middle
that how much U.S. production
is happening right now.

13:04.656 --> 13:05.646 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:05.786 --> 13:11.376 align:middle
So the first thing is, there
is the original thinking-the

13:11.916 --> 13:13.376 align:middle
long-standing thinking,
put it that way,

13:13.376 --> 13:14.976 align:middle
is that you do "look
through" energy shocks, but,

13:14.976 --> 13:16.206 align:middle
as I mentioned, that's
conditioned

13:16.206 --> 13:18.016 align:middle
on inflation expectations
and that kind of thing.

13:18.706 --> 13:20.186 align:middle
So, sorry, your question was?

13:20.416 --> 13:20.936 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:20.936 --> 13:23.006 align:middle
What the staff is saying
about, and has said

13:23.006 --> 13:23.906 align:middle
in the past- CHAIR POWELL.

13:24.166 --> 13:25.596 align:middle
So there'd be an offset.

13:25.596 --> 13:26.176 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:26.176 --> 13:26.566 align:middle
Offsets, yeah.

13:26.566 --> 13:26.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:26.986 --> 13:27.796 align:middle
So that's, that's true.

13:27.796 --> 13:30.736 align:middle
We have-we're a net
exporter of energy, right?

13:30.736 --> 13:33.636 align:middle
So any, any effects on
employment and economic activity

13:33.636 --> 13:37.466 align:middle
in spending would be offset
to some extent by the fact

13:37.466 --> 13:39.886 align:middle
that our, our oil companies
will be more profitable,

13:39.886 --> 13:41.246 align:middle
and they may even
do more drilling.

13:41.246 --> 13:43.926 align:middle
If you ask oil companies about
doing more drilling, though,

13:44.316 --> 13:50.846 align:middle
they're going to want to see a
consistent rise in oil prices

13:50.846 --> 13:54.706 align:middle
from where they were before
the buildup for the war.

13:55.316 --> 13:57.306 align:middle
And they're going to want
to believe that that's going

13:57.306 --> 14:00.306 align:middle
to be persistent for
a fairly long time.

14:00.306 --> 14:02.826 align:middle
So they're, they're not sitting
there waiting for oil to go

14:02.826 --> 14:05.486 align:middle
over $70 a barrel and then
they're going to March 18,

14:05.486 --> 14:07.486 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 8

14:07.486 --> 14:08.786 align:middle
of 25 start drilling
or whatever.

14:08.786 --> 14:10.636 align:middle
They're going to make a
reasoned, careful judgment

14:10.636 --> 14:12.266 align:middle
that we're going to
have higher oil prices

14:12.266 --> 14:14.106 align:middle
for an extended period of
time-meaningfully higher.

14:14.446 --> 14:16.916 align:middle
So you don't-I wouldn't say
there's much of that happening,

14:17.266 --> 14:19.386 align:middle
not much happening now.

14:19.456 --> 14:22.516 align:middle
But some of that could happen
over time if you see that.

14:22.836 --> 14:25.526 align:middle
So they're-the net-the
net of it-the net

14:25.526 --> 14:28.986 align:middle
of the oil shock will still
be some downward pressure

14:28.986 --> 14:32.206 align:middle
on spending and employment
and upward pressure

14:32.206 --> 14:33.056 align:middle
on inflation, of course.

14:33.056 --> 14:33.406 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

14:33.406 --> 14:35.646 align:middle
And if I could just follow
up on Colby's question,

14:36.126 --> 14:37.596 align:middle
how much do you worry
that having

14:37.596 --> 14:40.056 align:middle
"looked through" tariff
inflation and inflation running

14:40.056 --> 14:43.626 align:middle
above target and looking through
the oil price shock would

14:43.626 --> 14:45.956 align:middle
undermine the credibility
of your commitment

14:45.956 --> 14:46.886 align:middle
to the 2 percent target?

14:47.186 --> 14:47.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:47.516 --> 14:51.736 align:middle
You know, we have to do our
analysis and think these things

14:51.736 --> 14:54.206 align:middle
through carefully, but of course
that's on everyone's mind.

14:54.206 --> 14:58.456 align:middle
We're well aware of the
history and-but you don't want

14:58.456 --> 14:59.366 align:middle
to overreact to that.

14:59.366 --> 15:01.586 align:middle
You want to make the best
judgment you can based

15:01.586 --> 15:02.576 align:middle
on the facts.

15:02.646 --> 15:03.446 align:middle
I don't think we're going

15:03.446 --> 15:06.166 align:middle
to let it color our
decisionmaking more

15:06.166 --> 15:07.036 align:middle
than is appropriate.

15:07.556 --> 15:09.756 align:middle
It's more the thought
that it's been five years,

15:09.756 --> 15:11.816 align:middle
and we've actually had-we
had the tariff shock,

15:11.816 --> 15:16.206 align:middle
we had the pandemic, and
now we have an energy shock

15:16.206 --> 15:17.696 align:middle
of some size and duration.

15:17.696 --> 15:19.256 align:middle
We don't know what that's
going to be actually.

15:19.696 --> 15:23.026 align:middle
And it's one of those things
where it's, it's a repeated set

15:23.026 --> 15:25.816 align:middle
of things, and you worry
that that's the kind of thing

15:26.296 --> 15:29.536 align:middle
that can, can, you
know, cause trouble

15:29.536 --> 15:33.926 align:middle
for inflation expectations, and
so we worry a lot about that.

15:33.926 --> 15:38.566 align:middle
And we're-we are very strongly
committed to doing what it takes

15:38.566 --> 15:41.186 align:middle
to keep inflation expectations
anchored at 2 percent.

15:41.706 --> 15:45.366 align:middle
And I think it's important
that we-that we do that.

15:45.366 --> 15:45.966 align:middle
Very important.

15:45.966 --> 15:46.366 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:47.286 --> 15:50.526 align:middle
Nick. March 18, 2026 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

15:50.646 --> 15:52.956 align:middle
Page 9 of 25 NICK TIMIRAOS.

15:53.046 --> 15:54.496 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, Wall
Street Journal.

15:54.576 --> 15:56.666 align:middle
Chair Powell, you've
described monetary policy

15:56.666 --> 15:57.856 align:middle
as modestly restrictive.

15:58.036 --> 16:00.776 align:middle
If the unemployment
rate is roughly stable,

16:00.776 --> 16:03.816 align:middle
inflation moving
sideways in the high 2s,

16:04.296 --> 16:07.986 align:middle
with an ongoing overshoot in
services-nonhousing services,

16:07.986 --> 16:11.206 align:middle
not just goods-and the
economy doing fairly well

16:11.206 --> 16:14.166 align:middle
as you just outlined,
what gives you confidence

16:14.206 --> 16:17.386 align:middle
that inflation returns to target
over the next couple of years?

16:17.776 --> 16:18.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:18.066 --> 16:21.496 align:middle
Okay. So I would say the rate
is-you can characterize it

16:21.496 --> 16:25.016 align:middle
as in the high end of neutral,
or you can characterize it

16:25.016 --> 16:27.896 align:middle
as perhaps mildly restrictive,
even modestly restrictive.

16:28.316 --> 16:29.366 align:middle
No one knows for sure.

16:29.366 --> 16:31.626 align:middle
But it's in that range
where it's somewhere

16:31.626 --> 16:34.956 align:middle
around the borderline
between restrictive and not.

16:35.186 --> 16:38.906 align:middle
Remember that a big part of
the disinflation we're looking

16:38.906 --> 16:43.706 align:middle
for is just the runoff of-when
tariffs are put into place,

16:43.706 --> 16:46.796 align:middle
of course what they do is they
raise prices to some extent,

16:47.196 --> 16:49.786 align:middle
to the extent those prices
are-the tariffs are paid

16:49.786 --> 16:53.316 align:middle
by consumers on a
one-time basis.

16:53.466 --> 16:56.016 align:middle
And we're waiting for
that, that process.

16:56.426 --> 17:00.936 align:middle
It takes 8, 9, 10, 11 months-a
year-to go through the system.

17:00.936 --> 17:03.796 align:middle
And we're waiting for the
tariffs, which were put in place

17:03.876 --> 17:07.976 align:middle
over the course of the middle
part and later last year,

17:07.976 --> 17:10.026 align:middle
we're waiting for that
to go through the system

17:10.576 --> 17:13.466 align:middle
so that goods inflation
will return closer

17:13.466 --> 17:14.806 align:middle
to what it's always been.

17:14.806 --> 17:17.496 align:middle
I mean, it used to be-for many,
many years it was negative,

17:18.026 --> 17:21.466 align:middle
and then the year before
tariffs came in it was zero.

17:21.986 --> 17:23.856 align:middle
And it's running at,
like, 2 percent now, so,

17:24.306 --> 17:27.146 align:middle
so that's kind of-goods
inflation is running

17:27.146 --> 17:27.746 align:middle
at 2 percent.

17:27.746 --> 17:31.326 align:middle
So that's not coming from
standard, Phillips-curve,

17:31.436 --> 17:34.786 align:middle
restrictive, restrictive policy.

17:35.106 --> 17:39.016 align:middle
It's coming from the
runoff of a one-time thing.

17:39.556 --> 17:40.786 align:middle
We also think it's
important, though,

17:40.786 --> 17:44.616 align:middle
to keep policy either mildly
restrictive or close to that,

17:44.616 --> 17:47.976 align:middle
but not too restrictive because
of the weakness in-the downside

17:47.976 --> 17:48.946 align:middle
risk in the labor market.

17:49.276 --> 17:53.126 align:middle
We are balancing these two goals
in a situation where the risks

17:53.166 --> 17:55.416 align:middle
to the labor market are to
the downside-which would call

17:55.416 --> 17:58.176 align:middle
for lower rates-and the
risks to inflation are

17:58.176 --> 17:59.256 align:middle
to the upside-which March 18,

17:59.256 --> 18:01.156 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 10

18:01.156 --> 18:03.536 align:middle
of 25 would call for higher
rates, or not cutting, anyway.

18:03.536 --> 18:04.566 align:middle
So we're in a difficult
situation,

18:04.566 --> 18:06.246 align:middle
and we feel like we're-our
framework calls on us

18:06.246 --> 18:09.716 align:middle
to balance the risks, and we
feel like where we are now,

18:09.716 --> 18:12.766 align:middle
which is kind of on that
borderline-the higher borderline

18:12.766 --> 18:16.926 align:middle
of restrictive versus
not restrictive,

18:17.026 --> 18:18.556 align:middle
we feel like that's
the right place to be.

18:18.956 --> 18:19.486 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

18:19.486 --> 18:21.776 align:middle
Can I ask about core
nonhousing services, though,

18:21.826 --> 18:23.826 align:middle
because there's some people
who think that it hasn't come

18:23.826 --> 18:26.056 align:middle
down very much, if at
all, over the past year.

18:26.536 --> 18:29.706 align:middle
If wage growth has come down, if
the labor market has loosened,

18:30.126 --> 18:34.136 align:middle
why has nonhousing services
inflation maybe been slower

18:34.136 --> 18:34.726 align:middle
to follow?

18:34.726 --> 18:35.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:35.156 --> 18:36.106 align:middle
That's a good question.

18:37.146 --> 18:38.916 align:middle
It's, you know, it's
frustrating.

18:38.916 --> 18:40.756 align:middle
Labor-nonhousing services
[price increases] have,

18:40.836 --> 18:42.386 align:middle
they've basically moved
sideways for a year,

18:42.386 --> 18:43.306 align:middle
they're at the same level.

18:43.306 --> 18:46.826 align:middle
And we do expect that they will
come down, but it's a bunch

18:46.826 --> 18:47.966 align:middle
of idiosyncratic things.

18:48.006 --> 18:49.926 align:middle
But at the same time,
that is one

18:49.926 --> 18:51.756 align:middle
of the things we
should be seeing.

18:51.756 --> 18:53.236 align:middle
Because, to your
point, you know,

18:53.666 --> 18:56.276 align:middle
the labor market is
clearly not a source

18:56.276 --> 18:58.616 align:middle
of inflationary pressures,
and that should really matter

18:58.616 --> 18:59.926 align:middle
for nonhousing services.

19:00.396 --> 19:01.646 align:middle
But we're not seeing
progress there.

19:01.646 --> 19:05.906 align:middle
So we do-what we expect for next
year is continued-what we want

19:05.906 --> 19:08.626 align:middle
to see for this year,
rather, is continued progress

19:08.626 --> 19:13.496 align:middle
on housing services, finally
seeing the goods inflation come

19:13.496 --> 19:16.386 align:middle
back down, because the one-time
effects of tariffs are through,

19:16.386 --> 19:19.676 align:middle
and then also get some help
from nonhousing services.

19:19.676 --> 19:20.696 align:middle
That's what we'd like to see.

19:21.326 --> 19:23.546 align:middle
But it's, you know, it's a good
question why we didn't see much

19:23.546 --> 19:24.216 align:middle
of that last year.

19:26.066 --> 19:27.336 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:28.346 --> 19:28.896 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

19:29.066 --> 19:29.636 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

19:29.636 --> 19:31.166 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

19:31.166 --> 19:34.396 align:middle
So in December we saw
employment numbers revised

19:34.396 --> 19:37.596 align:middle
down to negative 17,000,
January revised down,

19:37.596 --> 19:39.726 align:middle
February posted a
loss of 92,000.

19:40.096 --> 19:43.146 align:middle
Is that-is the employment side a
far greater risk than March 18,

19:43.146 --> 19:45.396 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 11

19:45.396 --> 19:46.476 align:middle
of 25 the inflation side?

19:46.476 --> 19:47.646 align:middle
Because we are seeing
inflation-CPI is close

19:47.646 --> 19:49.046 align:middle
to 2 percent, PCE has
ticked down overall.

19:49.096 --> 19:49.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:49.886 --> 19:51.856 align:middle
You know, it's really-I
wouldn't say that.

19:51.856 --> 19:54.556 align:middle
I wouldn't say that that's
clear at all, that one is more

19:54.556 --> 19:55.356 align:middle
at risk than the other.

19:55.816 --> 19:59.746 align:middle
So you can-you can point to the
unemployment rate being stable,

19:59.746 --> 20:03.706 align:middle
and in a world where both supply
and demand for workers have come

20:03.706 --> 20:06.536 align:middle
down very, very sharply over
the course of the past year due

20:06.536 --> 20:10.646 align:middle
to immigration policy largely,
you know, a ratio is going

20:10.646 --> 20:14.076 align:middle
to be a better thing to look at
than job creation, for example.

20:14.336 --> 20:16.316 align:middle
And the ratio is the
unemployment rate,

20:16.316 --> 20:17.756 align:middle
and it's been stable
since September.

20:17.756 --> 20:19.156 align:middle
So that, that tells you that.

20:19.826 --> 20:24.696 align:middle
Inflation, you know,
you can-you can-we're

20:24.806 --> 20:29.906 align:middle
at 3.0 percent core
inflation, 2.8 percent headline,

20:30.206 --> 20:33.756 align:middle
so we've been well above
2 percent, that amount,

20:33.816 --> 20:38.406 align:middle
whatever it is, 0.7, 0.8,
we're a full percentage point

20:38.406 --> 20:39.916 align:middle
above 2 percent for some time.

20:40.556 --> 20:41.616 align:middle
And that's a concern.

20:41.616 --> 20:43.226 align:middle
We need to get back
down to 2 percent.

20:43.936 --> 20:45.666 align:middle
And we need to keep
focused on that,

20:45.666 --> 20:49.596 align:middle
even though we do now face
some new inflation from energy.

20:49.596 --> 20:52.166 align:middle
So I'd be hard pressed
to say that one

20:52.166 --> 20:54.096 align:middle
of them is obviously more
at risk than the other.

20:54.356 --> 20:54.836 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

20:54.836 --> 20:56.406 align:middle
And if I may, what happens

20:56.406 --> 21:00.856 align:middle
if there's no Federal Reserve
Chairman confirmed on May 15?

21:00.946 --> 21:01.426 align:middle
Would you stay on?

21:01.426 --> 21:02.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:02.266 --> 21:05.896 align:middle
So if my successor is not
confirmed by the end of my term

21:05.896 --> 21:09.756 align:middle
as Chair, I would serve as Chair
pro tem until he is confirmed.

21:09.926 --> 21:14.886 align:middle
That is what the law calls
for, that's what we've done

21:14.886 --> 21:17.466 align:middle
on several occasions-including
involving me-and that's what

21:17.466 --> 21:18.596 align:middle
we're going to do
in this situation.

21:19.316 --> 21:22.336 align:middle
And while I'm at it,

21:22.646 --> 21:25.216 align:middle
on the question whether I will
leave while the investigation is

21:25.216 --> 21:27.806 align:middle
ongoing: I have no
intention of leaving the Board

21:27.806 --> 21:30.396 align:middle
until the investigation
is well and truly over,

21:30.746 --> 21:34.106 align:middle
with transparency and
finality, and I would refer you

21:34.106 --> 21:36.046 align:middle
to the statement that
was in the Fed's brief

21:36.046 --> 21:36.976 align:middle
that you all have seen.

21:36.976 --> 21:38.136 align:middle
And I won't have anything
more for you on that.

21:38.136 --> 21:40.006 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 12

21:40.006 --> 21:42.056 align:middle
of 25 On the question of whether
I will then continue to serve

21:42.056 --> 21:44.066 align:middle
as a Governor after my term ends

21:44.176 --> 21:45.686 align:middle
and after the investigation
is over,

21:46.146 --> 21:47.676 align:middle
I have not made that
decision yet.

21:48.316 --> 21:50.996 align:middle
And I will make that decision
based on what I think is best

21:50.996 --> 21:54.536 align:middle
for the institution and for the
people we serve-figuring you

21:54.536 --> 21:58.976 align:middle
probably were going to set the
dominoes off there, Edward.

21:58.976 --> 22:00.906 align:middle
And I'm not going to
have any more to say

22:00.906 --> 22:01.976 align:middle
on those issues, by the way.

22:03.476 --> 22:04.156 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

22:05.806 --> 22:06.476 align:middle
Thank you very much.

22:06.476 --> 22:08.416 align:middle
Claire Jones from
the Financial Times.

22:08.416 --> 22:12.576 align:middle
People have drawn quite a
few historical parallels

22:12.576 --> 22:14.796 align:middle
with the current situation-we've
seen oil price shocks

22:14.796 --> 22:15.246 align:middle
in the past.

22:15.936 --> 22:19.356 align:middle
Some people have said the first
Gulf War and the Fed's response

22:19.436 --> 22:22.676 align:middle
to that, where there was a
focus on the growth risks,

22:23.436 --> 22:24.556 align:middle
is the right parallel.

22:24.586 --> 22:27.576 align:middle
To what extent would
you agree with that,

22:27.576 --> 22:28.806 align:middle
and to what extent
would you think we're

22:28.806 --> 22:30.216 align:middle
in a different place today?

22:30.836 --> 22:31.156 align:middle
Thank you.

22:31.966 --> 22:32.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:32.266 --> 22:34.146 align:middle
You know, we-it's
really hard to say

22:34.146 --> 22:36.526 align:middle
until we see the situation,
the actual situation.

22:36.716 --> 22:40.996 align:middle
In some circumstances that
might be the right case.

22:40.996 --> 22:43.096 align:middle
In fact, for example,
if we do see the kinds

22:43.326 --> 22:47.226 align:middle
of disinflationary progress
that we expect to see with-due

22:47.226 --> 22:48.506 align:middle
to tariffs that I mentioned.

22:49.136 --> 22:50.486 align:middle
I think it's hard to say.

22:50.486 --> 22:54.076 align:middle
And it will depend, to some
extent, on the size and duration

22:54.076 --> 22:57.236 align:middle
of the effects we're seeing on
prices, and it will depend a lot

22:57.406 --> 23:00.776 align:middle
on what we can see in
inflation expectations.

23:01.466 --> 23:01.986 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

23:01.986 --> 23:05.156 align:middle
And just one follow-up, if I
may, just on the BLS report

23:05.276 --> 23:06.696 align:middle
that was mentioned
briefly there.

23:07.046 --> 23:08.986 align:middle
It doesn't seem from
the projections,

23:08.986 --> 23:10.456 align:middle
or from the comments
you've made today

23:10.456 --> 23:13.776 align:middle
about the unemployment rate and
focusing on that, that your view

23:13.776 --> 23:15.366 align:middle
on the labor market story

23:15.856 --> 23:18.716 align:middle
to a large part being a
supply-side story has changed,

23:19.296 --> 23:22.306 align:middle
despite the quite negative
number in February.

23:22.306 --> 23:24.086 align:middle
Would that be the right take?

23:24.086 --> 23:27.026 align:middle
And was there anyone else in the
room who thought differently,

23:27.026 --> 23:30.386 align:middle
in that the February jobs
report was cause for concern?

23:30.426 --> 23:34.506 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 13

23:34.506 --> 23:35.226 align:middle
of 25 CHAIR POWELL.

23:35.226 --> 23:36.406 align:middle
So I think you have to
take the two together.

23:36.406 --> 23:40.906 align:middle
In a way, the January report
was a positive surprise,

23:40.906 --> 23:43.016 align:middle
and the February report
was a negative surprise.

23:43.016 --> 23:47.296 align:middle
If you put them together, you
get something in the middle,

23:47.356 --> 23:49.546 align:middle
and I think you have to
really be-as you do, I'm sure,

23:49.546 --> 23:52.616 align:middle
realize there was a strike
and there was weather,

23:52.616 --> 23:55.726 align:middle
and that's about 80,000 of
the negative effect total

23:56.316 --> 23:57.476 align:middle
on the February report.

23:58.116 --> 24:00.536 align:middle
Notwithstanding that,
so what do you see

24:00.536 --> 24:01.726 align:middle
if you take a step back overall?

24:01.726 --> 24:02.806 align:middle
There are a number of indicators

24:02.806 --> 24:04.756 align:middle
that suggest a degree
of stability.

24:05.376 --> 24:08.936 align:middle
But the thing that I think
a good number of the people

24:08.936 --> 24:11.656 align:middle
on the Committee are concerned
about is just the very,

24:11.656 --> 24:13.406 align:middle
very low level of job creation.

24:13.796 --> 24:17.576 align:middle
If you adjust what has
been the trend job creation

24:17.576 --> 24:20.196 align:middle
over the past, let's say,
six months, if you adjust

24:20.196 --> 24:22.586 align:middle
that for what we
think, our staff thinks,

24:22.636 --> 24:25.496 align:middle
is the overstatement
due to overcounting,

24:25.806 --> 24:28.886 align:middle
effectively there's zero net job
creation in the private sector.

24:29.586 --> 24:32.016 align:middle
But actually that
looks like that's

24:32.016 --> 24:35.886 align:middle
about what the economy needs
in terms of dealing with very,

24:35.886 --> 24:38.656 align:middle
very low-nonexistent,
really-growth

24:38.656 --> 24:39.996 align:middle
in the labor force,
which, of course,

24:39.996 --> 24:41.426 align:middle
we've never had in our history.

24:41.966 --> 24:46.886 align:middle
So you've got a sort of a zero
employment growth equilibrium.

24:47.756 --> 24:53.386 align:middle
Now that's balance, okay, but
I would say it does have a feel

24:53.386 --> 24:56.226 align:middle
of downside risk,
and it's not kind

24:56.226 --> 24:57.666 align:middle
of a really comfortable balance.

24:57.666 --> 25:00.816 align:middle
So we look at that,
we see it, we get it.

25:00.816 --> 25:02.706 align:middle
I mean, everybody
understands the arithmetic

25:02.706 --> 25:05.876 align:middle
and that's-you can say
the breakeven is zero.

25:06.696 --> 25:07.786 align:middle
But, nonetheless, it's,

25:08.346 --> 25:10.096 align:middle
it's something we're
watching carefully

25:10.706 --> 25:12.136 align:middle
and are concerned about.

25:12.196 --> 25:15.876 align:middle
But ultimately, though, you can
argue that it's a consequence

25:15.876 --> 25:18.546 align:middle
of deliberate policy,
which is really the changes

25:18.546 --> 25:22.246 align:middle
to immigration-that's the
biggest factor there-but,

25:23.126 --> 25:24.636 align:middle
nonetheless, something
we watch carefully.

25:25.966 --> 25:26.356 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:27.306 --> 25:29.246 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

25:29.756 --> 25:30.676 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

25:30.676 --> 25:34.266 align:middle
The economy has experienced
a series of supply shocks

25:34.266 --> 25:37.546 align:middle
in the last few years: COVID,
tariffs, two oil price shocks.

25:37.546 --> 25:38.296 align:middle
Do you think that March 18,

25:38.296 --> 25:40.146 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 14

25:40.146 --> 25:41.556 align:middle
of 25 is bad luck, or has
something changed in the world

25:41.556 --> 25:43.116 align:middle
that makes supply
shocks more common?

25:43.116 --> 25:46.136 align:middle
And does the central bank need
to take-start taking account

25:46.176 --> 25:48.706 align:middle
of the risk of supply shocks
as a more common problem?

25:48.706 --> 25:49.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:50.826 --> 25:52.776 align:middle
You know, we did go
through a long period

25:52.776 --> 25:57.296 align:middle
where the shocks were all demand
shocks, and so we've had a lot

25:57.296 --> 25:59.886 align:middle
of practice thinking about
supply shocks in the last four

25:59.886 --> 26:00.966 align:middle
or five years, for sure.

26:01.476 --> 26:03.996 align:middle
And it's just a very different
thing-much more difficult

26:03.996 --> 26:06.706 align:middle
thing-because it does
immediately raise the question

26:07.396 --> 26:09.966 align:middle
of tension between the
two parts of our mandate.

26:10.746 --> 26:12.736 align:middle
But has the world changed?

26:12.736 --> 26:17.466 align:middle
I mean, COVID, COVID is
a one-time thing, right?

26:17.496 --> 26:20.796 align:middle
This energy supply shock
is a one-time thing.

26:20.796 --> 26:23.276 align:middle
It's not-it's not because
of some broad tendency

26:23.276 --> 26:25.436 align:middle
or anything, I don't think.

26:25.436 --> 26:28.986 align:middle
And the oil shock under-with
Ukraine was also a consequence

26:29.226 --> 26:31.126 align:middle
of military action.

26:31.126 --> 26:36.256 align:middle
So I don't know that-I don't
know that the world has changed

26:36.256 --> 26:37.836 align:middle
in a way that there'll
be more supply shocks,

26:38.106 --> 26:40.286 align:middle
but there's-people
have written that paper

26:40.286 --> 26:42.446 align:middle
and that speech a
number of times.

26:42.446 --> 26:43.826 align:middle
A number of people have
tried to make the case

26:43.826 --> 26:44.676 align:middle
that that is the case.

26:44.676 --> 26:47.306 align:middle
And, in fact, we have
seen more supply shocks

26:48.106 --> 26:49.666 align:middle
in the last five
years than we've seen

26:49.666 --> 26:50.896 align:middle
in many years before that.

26:51.116 --> 26:51.676 align:middle
It's a fact.

26:52.256 --> 26:53.446 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

26:53.866 --> 26:54.836 align:middle
Okay. You said last year

26:54.836 --> 26:57.326 align:middle
that the Fed was reviewing
its communication strategies,

26:57.326 --> 26:59.926 align:middle
including the SEP, as part
of its framework review.

27:00.326 --> 27:01.806 align:middle
What ever happened to that?

27:02.046 --> 27:06.056 align:middle
And I have, like, five
follow-ups to that, but,

27:06.056 --> 27:09.186 align:middle
I guess, what would you change

27:09.186 --> 27:10.956 align:middle
about Fed communications
if you had the time?

27:10.956 --> 27:11.346 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

27:11.346 --> 27:15.026 align:middle
What happened about that was
not much, and I'll tell you why.

27:15.026 --> 27:19.996 align:middle
So, you know, we, we looked
carefully at many aspects

27:19.996 --> 27:21.706 align:middle
of the SEP and of
our communications,

27:22.316 --> 27:24.816 align:middle
and there just weren't-there
weren't any ideas

27:24.856 --> 27:27.426 align:middle
that had very broad
support on the Committee.

27:27.426 --> 27:29.676 align:middle
And you don't want
to make a change

27:29.676 --> 27:30.946 align:middle
to your communications
unless you have,

27:31.536 --> 27:32.856 align:middle
have the Committee behind that.

27:32.856 --> 27:35.456 align:middle
So we didn't really make
any changes on that.

27:35.456 --> 27:36.426 align:middle
I had hoped-had some
things that I March 18,

27:36.426 --> 27:38.676 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 15

27:38.676 --> 27:40.686 align:middle
of 25 wanted to do, but they
just-they didn't attract broad

27:40.686 --> 27:42.866 align:middle
support, and we'd already
made all the changes we made

27:42.866 --> 27:44.996 align:middle
to the framework-that
was a critical thing.

27:45.716 --> 27:47.006 align:middle
So we didn't.

27:47.086 --> 27:52.326 align:middle
And I wish we had been able to
do some things, but we didn't.

27:52.986 --> 27:54.876 align:middle
So maybe the next
Chair will take a look.

27:54.876 --> 27:55.666 align:middle
I'm sure he will.

27:57.396 --> 27:58.436 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:59.056 --> 27:59.936 align:middle
Mike McKee.

28:00.486 --> 28:01.306 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

28:02.386 --> 28:04.206 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

28:04.516 --> 28:07.376 align:middle
The minutes show in January some
members of the Committee wanted

28:07.376 --> 28:10.846 align:middle
to include a two-sided
guidance on policy.

28:10.926 --> 28:12.286 align:middle
Was that discussed today?

28:12.286 --> 28:16.076 align:middle
And given the rise in the
inflation expectations,

28:16.076 --> 28:20.516 align:middle
how much support for a two-sided
policy warning would there be?

28:20.516 --> 28:21.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:21.606 --> 28:22.826 align:middle
So it did come up today.

28:22.826 --> 28:25.216 align:middle
The policy that our-the
possibility, rather,

28:25.216 --> 28:27.246 align:middle
that our next move might
be an increase did come

28:27.246 --> 28:28.966 align:middle
up at the meeting, as it
did at the last meeting.

28:29.546 --> 28:32.096 align:middle
The vast majority of
participants don't see

28:32.096 --> 28:34.526 align:middle
that as their base
case, and, of course,

28:34.526 --> 28:36.306 align:middle
we don't take things
off the table.

28:36.926 --> 28:39.726 align:middle
But you correctly
characterized what was

28:39.726 --> 28:42.726 align:middle
in the minutes-that several
participants indicated something

28:42.726 --> 28:44.646 align:middle
very much like that
conversation did happen.

28:44.646 --> 28:45.976 align:middle
What was your second question?

28:46.196 --> 28:46.726 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

28:46.726 --> 28:48.046 align:middle
Oh, it was just one
question there.

28:48.106 --> 28:51.906 align:middle
But a follow-up would be, there
are a lot of goods besides oil

28:52.236 --> 28:53.756 align:middle
that are trapped in
the Strait right now,

28:53.756 --> 28:55.996 align:middle
and a lot of supply
chains getting snarled.

28:56.446 --> 28:59.086 align:middle
How much concern do you have

28:59.086 --> 29:04.086 align:middle
about whether-if this becomes
an inflation problem beyond just

29:04.086 --> 29:06.666 align:middle
oil, whether there's
anything you can do about it,

29:06.896 --> 29:11.676 align:middle
or would do about it, given the
efficacies of monetary policy?

29:13.366 --> 29:13.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:13.686 --> 29:16.726 align:middle
You know, we-you can-you can
worry about other commodities,

29:16.726 --> 29:20.776 align:middle
you can worry about all of
the ways that oil commodities,

29:21.076 --> 29:22.296 align:middle
oil-related commodities go

29:22.296 --> 29:23.606 align:middle
into manufacturing,
that kind of thing.

29:24.076 --> 29:26.476 align:middle
The truth is, it's completely
out of our hands, and we just,

29:26.686 --> 29:31.336 align:middle
like March 18, 2026 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

29:31.336 --> 29:33.056 align:middle
Page 16 of 25 everybody else,

29:33.056 --> 29:34.986 align:middle
we have to just wait
and see what happens.

29:34.986 --> 29:36.416 align:middle
And it'll be-it'll come
down to how long, you know,

29:36.706 --> 29:39.976 align:middle
the current situation lasts,
and then what are the effects

29:40.066 --> 29:43.676 align:middle
on prices and then
how do consumers react

29:43.676 --> 29:44.336 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

29:44.336 --> 29:47.016 align:middle
Really, we-I wouldn't
speculate in any way,

29:47.016 --> 29:48.776 align:middle
and there's really not
a lot we can do other

29:48.776 --> 29:50.986 align:middle
than kind of watch and see.

29:50.986 --> 29:51.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:53.656 --> 29:53.966 align:middle
Maria Eloisa.

29:53.966 --> 29:56.766 align:middle
MARIA ELOISA CAPURRO.

29:56.766 --> 29:57.586 align:middle
Afternoon, Chair Powell.

29:57.676 --> 29:59.366 align:middle
Maria Eloisa Capurro
with Bloomberg News.

30:00.096 --> 30:02.926 align:middle
I wanted to ask you, you
said longer-term measures

30:02.996 --> 30:06.276 align:middle
of inflation expectations
reflect confidence

30:06.276 --> 30:08.616 align:middle
that the Fed will get back
to its 2 percent goal.

30:08.976 --> 30:11.046 align:middle
But there are some colleagues
of yours who have pointed

30:11.046 --> 30:13.526 align:middle
to higher dispersion
in household service

30:13.526 --> 30:17.226 align:middle
and business service as
well, so-and that they take

30:17.226 --> 30:20.846 align:middle
as a signal that inflation
expectations may be less sticky

30:20.966 --> 30:21.426 align:middle
than in the past.

30:21.426 --> 30:24.856 align:middle
I'm wondering if there was
a discussion around that

30:24.926 --> 30:26.646 align:middle
at this meeting,
across the Committee.

30:26.976 --> 30:29.866 align:middle
And what were the
views-what is the state

30:29.866 --> 30:32.546 align:middle
of inflation expectations
and the risks

30:32.546 --> 30:35.126 align:middle
from higher oil and gas prices?

30:35.156 --> 30:35.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:35.786 --> 30:40.926 align:middle
So at this meeting-I mean, a
number of people mentioned,

30:40.926 --> 30:44.626 align:middle
and staff briefed on, short-term
expectations having moved

30:44.626 --> 30:46.906 align:middle
up quite a lot for
reasons we understand well.

30:47.346 --> 30:48.936 align:middle
But long-term expectations,
you know,

30:48.936 --> 30:52.296 align:middle
you can always find one that's
troubling or some aspect

30:52.296 --> 30:54.756 align:middle
of one that's troubling,
but ultimately right

30:54.756 --> 30:58.956 align:middle
through this whole period,
the overwhelming majority

30:58.956 --> 31:01.496 align:middle
of the things we look
to, including markets,

31:01.496 --> 31:05.246 align:middle
including surveys of, you
know, the public and also

31:05.736 --> 31:08.866 align:middle
of forecasters, they've
all been pretty solid

31:08.866 --> 31:12.716 align:middle
on longer-term inflation
expectations being right

31:12.716 --> 31:14.456 align:middle
where they need to be,
consistent with 2 percent.

31:15.096 --> 31:17.046 align:middle
So that continues
to be the case.

31:17.046 --> 31:19.946 align:middle
We didn't-we didn't have a lot
of conversation about that.

31:19.946 --> 31:21.366 align:middle
I think everyone does agree

31:21.906 --> 31:24.916 align:middle
that we'll be watching
those extremely carefully

31:25.336 --> 31:27.716 align:middle
as we see the effects of
the price increases come

31:27.716 --> 31:28.696 align:middle
through from the conflict.

31:28.696 --> 31:31.936 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 17

31:31.936 --> 31:33.096 align:middle
of 25 MARIA ELOISA CAPURRO.

31:33.096 --> 31:35.046 align:middle
And still on how the discussion
went across the Committee,

31:35.046 --> 31:38.126 align:middle
before coming to this meeting
there were some members

31:38.126 --> 31:42.386 align:middle
who were, like, discussing the
risks of a slowdown in growth

31:43.436 --> 31:45.876 align:middle
at a time where inflation
remains high.

31:45.986 --> 31:49.506 align:middle
It really doesn't seem that
most members are penciling

31:49.506 --> 31:51.036 align:middle
in a slowdown in growth.

31:51.646 --> 31:54.536 align:middle
But has there been any
discussion of the risks

31:54.686 --> 31:56.446 align:middle
of stagflation at this point?

31:56.526 --> 31:57.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:57.346 --> 32:00.626 align:middle
So people actually wrote
up their growth forecast

32:00.626 --> 32:03.006 align:middle
by a tenth, and I think
that's probably to do

32:03.006 --> 32:06.786 align:middle
with just growing
confidence in productivity.

32:07.656 --> 32:11.056 align:middle
But so-sorry, and your
second question was?

32:11.056 --> 32:13.106 align:middle
MARIA ELOISA CAPURRO.

32:13.786 --> 32:16.046 align:middle
If there have been any
discussions about the risk

32:16.046 --> 32:17.326 align:middle
of stagflation- CHAIR POWELL.

32:17.326 --> 32:18.006 align:middle
Of stagflation.

32:18.066 --> 32:20.986 align:middle
Yeah, so as I mentioned,
there's tension

32:20.986 --> 32:22.516 align:middle
between the two goals, right?

32:22.746 --> 32:24.786 align:middle
The upward risk for inflation

32:24.786 --> 32:26.846 align:middle
and the downward
risk for employment.

32:27.266 --> 32:28.876 align:middle
And that puts us in a
different situation.

32:29.466 --> 32:32.256 align:middle
You know, when we use the term
"stagflation," I always have

32:32.256 --> 32:35.966 align:middle
to point out that that
was a 1970s term at a time

32:35.966 --> 32:37.806 align:middle
when unemployment was
in double figures,

32:37.916 --> 32:39.256 align:middle
and inflation was really high,

32:39.256 --> 32:41.726 align:middle
and the misery index was
super high-add them together,

32:42.206 --> 32:43.216 align:middle
you get the misery index.

32:43.706 --> 32:44.816 align:middle
And that's not the
case right now.

32:44.936 --> 32:46.886 align:middle
We actually have
unemployment really close

32:46.886 --> 32:50.596 align:middle
to longer-run normal, and we
have inflation that's, you know,

32:50.596 --> 32:52.566 align:middle
1 percentage point above that.

32:52.566 --> 32:54.296 align:middle
So calling that "stagflation,"

32:54.806 --> 32:57.826 align:middle
it's not-I would reserve
the term "stagflation"

32:57.826 --> 33:01.516 align:middle
for a much more serious
set of circumstances.

33:01.516 --> 33:03.126 align:middle
That is not the situation
we're in.

33:03.126 --> 33:06.116 align:middle
What we have is some
tension between the goals,

33:06.536 --> 33:08.016 align:middle
and we're trying to
manage our way through it.

33:08.576 --> 33:10.606 align:middle
It's a very difficult
situation, but it's nothing

33:10.606 --> 33:12.856 align:middle
like what they faced
in the 1970s,

33:12.856 --> 33:14.736 align:middle
and that's-I reserve stagflation

33:14.736 --> 33:16.586 align:middle
for that-the word-for
that period.

33:16.586 --> 33:17.296 align:middle
Maybe that's just me.

33:17.296 --> 33:17.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:18.146 --> 33:18.386 align:middle
Elizabeth.

33:18.386 --> 33:23.026 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 18

33:23.026 --> 33:23.936 align:middle
of 25 ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

33:23.936 --> 33:24.296 align:middle
Thanks much.

33:24.296 --> 33:25.496 align:middle
Elizabeth Schulze with ABC News.

33:25.496 --> 33:27.476 align:middle
President Trump says
that oil prices

33:27.476 --> 33:30.326 align:middle
and overall inflation
will go down very rapidly

33:30.326 --> 33:31.526 align:middle
as soon as the war ends.

33:31.606 --> 33:32.776 align:middle
Do you agree with that?

33:33.366 --> 33:33.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:33.586 --> 33:35.306 align:middle
I don't have a forecast on that.

33:35.856 --> 33:36.396 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

33:36.396 --> 33:39.046 align:middle
We're thinking about the
impact on American households.

33:39.046 --> 33:41.736 align:middle
As you know, they've been facing
these higher prices for years,

33:41.786 --> 33:44.026 align:middle
they've seen an almost
$1 increase in gas.

33:44.796 --> 33:46.366 align:middle
How worried are you
about households,

33:46.366 --> 33:47.996 align:middle
especially lower-income
families,

33:48.036 --> 33:49.876 align:middle
being able to afford
those price hikes,

33:50.346 --> 33:52.496 align:middle
and should people be
bracing for higher costs,

33:52.496 --> 33:53.566 align:middle
including for food, now?

33:54.866 --> 33:55.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:55.306 --> 33:57.416 align:middle
We just-we don't know how big
these effects are going to be,

33:57.416 --> 34:00.096 align:middle
and I don't-I don't want to
characterize them in any way.

34:00.096 --> 34:03.556 align:middle
Of course, people are already
seeing gas prices-gas prices are

34:03.556 --> 34:05.966 align:middle
up almost a dollar a gallon.

34:06.156 --> 34:08.696 align:middle
We hope that isn't for
a long period of time,

34:08.696 --> 34:10.256 align:middle
and of course people
will feel that.

34:10.376 --> 34:12.596 align:middle
But I don't want to
speculate about what

34:12.596 --> 34:15.036 align:middle
that might mean because,
honestly, that would sound

34:15.036 --> 34:17.276 align:middle
like I have some idea of what's
actually going to happen,

34:17.276 --> 34:18.536 align:middle
and I don't think
anybody really does.

34:18.776 --> 34:20.586 align:middle
We're going to see
how this works out,

34:21.226 --> 34:22.846 align:middle
and I just would
leave it at that.

34:22.846 --> 34:24.706 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:24.706 --> 34:27.156 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

34:29.666 --> 34:31.146 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

34:31.146 --> 34:35.666 align:middle
I just wanted to ask, for
purposes of the next meeting,

34:36.256 --> 34:39.166 align:middle
how the Middle East war
develops between now and then,

34:39.166 --> 34:41.776 align:middle
how are you thinking about
what might guide action

34:41.776 --> 34:42.496 align:middle
at your next meeting?

34:42.496 --> 34:45.586 align:middle
I mean, if, if we have oil
prices above, you know,

34:45.826 --> 34:49.626 align:middle
$100 a barrel the entire
time until the next meeting,

34:49.986 --> 34:52.356 align:middle
is that going to change
your wait-and-see stance?

34:52.426 --> 34:56.626 align:middle
Like, what, what would lead you
all to move or is-or are you

34:56.866 --> 34:59.216 align:middle
on hold pretty indefinitely?

34:59.216 --> 35:02.766 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 19

35:02.766 --> 35:04.186 align:middle
of 25 CHAIR POWELL.

35:04.186 --> 35:04.706 align:middle
We'll have to wait and see.

35:04.706 --> 35:06.216 align:middle
I mean, we always say
we're going to learn more

35:06.216 --> 35:09.156 align:middle
by the next meeting,
and usually we do.

35:09.156 --> 35:11.406 align:middle
But in this case, we're going to
learn a lot, because we're going

35:11.406 --> 35:15.076 align:middle
to learn-six weeks to the
day until the next meeting.

35:15.496 --> 35:18.196 align:middle
And we're going to see whether
the-what happens-it's going

35:18.316 --> 35:21.126 align:middle
to be very important for
the way the economy looks

35:21.126 --> 35:24.626 align:middle
and the way the outlook-the
way the outlook evolves

35:25.236 --> 35:26.196 align:middle
with what happens
in the Middle East.

35:26.196 --> 35:28.356 align:middle
That's going to be a big factor,
and we'll know that then.

35:28.356 --> 35:29.766 align:middle
I don't know how it's going
to affect our thinking.

35:29.766 --> 35:30.486 align:middle
I really don't.

35:30.606 --> 35:32.996 align:middle
And we did-we did talk

35:32.996 --> 35:34.706 align:middle
about alternative
scenarios a little bit,

35:34.706 --> 35:36.916 align:middle
but it's just-I wouldn't
bring that in here.

35:37.076 --> 35:40.876 align:middle
It's very uncertain, and I
just want to-just remember

35:40.876 --> 35:43.906 align:middle
that we don't know, and we
shouldn't assume it's going

35:43.906 --> 35:45.016 align:middle
to be one thing or another.

35:45.196 --> 35:45.566 align:middle
We're going to see.

35:45.566 --> 35:46.176 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

35:46.426 --> 35:50.356 align:middle
So is it a matter of you don't
know how the situation itself is

35:50.356 --> 35:53.646 align:middle
going to evolve, or even if
things remain as they are,

35:53.646 --> 35:55.936 align:middle
the economy could still
remain relatively durable

35:55.936 --> 35:56.596 align:middle
in the face of them?

35:56.596 --> 35:56.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:57.326 --> 35:58.426 align:middle
Yeah, that's true too.

35:58.596 --> 36:04.926 align:middle
No, I mean, the U.S. economy
has just made-it's been strong

36:05.016 --> 36:07.666 align:middle
through a whole bunch
of challenges.

36:07.666 --> 36:09.946 align:middle
I mean, if you go
back-go back to 2023

36:09.946 --> 36:13.746 align:middle
when we raised rates
a lot, in '22 and '23,

36:14.316 --> 36:16.696 align:middle
and close to 100 percent
of economists called

36:16.696 --> 36:18.616 align:middle
for a recession,
which didn't happen.

36:18.616 --> 36:21.396 align:middle
In fact, 2023 was a
really strong year.

36:21.396 --> 36:26.606 align:middle
So the U.S. economy
has really been just,

36:27.896 --> 36:32.016 align:middle
just doing pretty well through
a lot of significant challenges

36:32.016 --> 36:32.896 align:middle
over the past few years.

36:32.896 --> 36:33.886 align:middle
It's been amazing to see.

36:34.626 --> 36:37.896 align:middle
And, again, I don't-I don't
know what's going to happen

36:38.136 --> 36:40.836 align:middle
in the next intermeeting cycle,
and I don't know what's going

36:40.836 --> 36:41.686 align:middle
to happen in the Middle East.

36:41.686 --> 36:42.576 align:middle
I wouldn't want to speculate.

36:43.226 --> 36:44.056 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:44.266 --> 36:44.466 align:middle
Courtenay.

36:44.696 --> 36:49.716 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 20

36:49.716 --> 36:50.656 align:middle
of 25 COURTENAY BROWN.

36:50.656 --> 36:51.006 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

36:51.006 --> 36:52.086 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

36:52.126 --> 36:53.256 align:middle
What makes you so certain

36:53.386 --> 36:57.306 align:middle
that tariff-related
price increases will be a

36:57.306 --> 36:58.456 align:middle
one-time effect?

36:58.456 --> 36:59.536 align:middle
I don't think we've seen you

36:59.536 --> 37:03.726 align:middle
since the Supreme Court tariff
decision, and it's pretty clear

37:03.726 --> 37:06.176 align:middle
that none of us know
what the extent

37:06.176 --> 37:08.596 align:middle
of the tariff shock will be
as the Administration moves

37:08.646 --> 37:12.086 align:middle
to replace some of the
tariffs that were overturned.

37:12.086 --> 37:13.606 align:middle
So I suppose I'm curious,

37:13.606 --> 37:16.716 align:middle
what would make you
question this certainty

37:16.716 --> 37:18.996 align:middle
that tariffs are going to
be a one-time price effect?

37:19.086 --> 37:19.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:19.956 --> 37:22.466 align:middle
Well, I, I would not at
all use the word "certain"

37:22.516 --> 37:24.346 align:middle
about my views on that.

37:24.346 --> 37:26.066 align:middle
I'm not certain; I'm uncertain.

37:26.586 --> 37:29.386 align:middle
But just, if you think
about what it is,

37:29.526 --> 37:33.366 align:middle
it's a one-time increase in
the price of a good, right?

37:33.836 --> 37:39.406 align:middle
And what inflation is is ongoing
increases in prices this year,

37:39.406 --> 37:40.376 align:middle
next year, the year after.

37:40.376 --> 37:41.386 align:middle
That's what inflation is.

37:41.386 --> 37:42.856 align:middle
It's not a one-time
price increase.

37:42.856 --> 37:44.806 align:middle
There's a very, very
big difference.

37:44.806 --> 37:46.336 align:middle
The public doesn't
really focus on that.

37:46.366 --> 37:47.686 align:middle
But that's what-that's
the difference.

37:47.686 --> 37:52.526 align:middle
And tariffs should be, in
theory, unless they cause people

37:52.526 --> 37:55.516 align:middle
to start expecting still
more tariffs the next year

37:55.516 --> 37:57.026 align:middle
and still more tariffs
the next year,

37:57.686 --> 37:59.936 align:middle
they should be a
classic one-time thing.

37:59.996 --> 38:01.696 align:middle
People say the same
thing, traditionally,

38:01.696 --> 38:04.196 align:middle
about an energy price spike
because, traditionally,

38:04.456 --> 38:05.886 align:middle
prices go up and
they come back down.

38:06.296 --> 38:11.216 align:middle
And by the time monetary policy
would react, it would be over.

38:11.216 --> 38:13.686 align:middle
So I don't have tons
of confidence on that.

38:13.686 --> 38:16.626 align:middle
I mean, I think the
theory is probably right.

38:16.966 --> 38:20.646 align:middle
But, as usual, the time it
takes to get all the way

38:20.646 --> 38:22.516 align:middle
through the economy is
just very uncertain.

38:22.936 --> 38:24.806 align:middle
And, you know, we found
that coming out of COVID,

38:24.806 --> 38:26.726 align:middle
that the inflation did go away,

38:26.726 --> 38:28.776 align:middle
and largely for the
reasons we thought it would.

38:29.166 --> 38:31.776 align:middle
But it took two years
longer than we thought.

38:31.776 --> 38:34.886 align:middle
And so I think we
have to be humble

38:34.886 --> 38:38.626 align:middle
about knowing how long
it will take for tariffs

38:38.626 --> 38:40.386 align:middle
to go all the way
through the economy.

38:40.386 --> 38:42.786 align:middle
And so what we've been
doing is-or our staff's been

38:42.786 --> 38:44.626 align:middle
doing-it's very interesting.

38:44.626 --> 38:45.876 align:middle
They started off with
just an estimate,

38:45.876 --> 38:46.866 align:middle
because there wasn't
a real history.

38:46.866 --> 38:49.566 align:middle
And then March 18, 2026 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

38:49.566 --> 38:51.496 align:middle
Page 21 of 25 they built,
based on the history

38:51.496 --> 38:54.306 align:middle
that they're seeing of tariffs
coming through into prices,

38:55.416 --> 38:57.456 align:middle
they've now-they
can now have an arc

38:58.076 --> 39:01.976 align:middle
and for all the tariffs they can
say, well-so we have, I think,

39:01.976 --> 39:06.386 align:middle
slightly more confidence that
we will see tariff inflation

39:06.386 --> 39:06.996 align:middle
coming down.

39:06.996 --> 39:09.766 align:middle
Not prices, but you won't
see further increases,

39:10.026 --> 39:12.906 align:middle
and we should start
seeing that more and more

39:12.906 --> 39:14.046 align:middle
in the middle parts of the year.

39:14.046 --> 39:15.016 align:middle
We do expect that.

39:15.506 --> 39:20.196 align:middle
Now, you're also right, though,
that the level of tariffs came

39:20.196 --> 39:24.206 align:middle
down fairly meaningfully in
the wake of the-in the wake

39:24.206 --> 39:25.066 align:middle
of the Court decision.

39:25.066 --> 39:26.886 align:middle
But the Administration
said they're going

39:26.976 --> 39:28.546 align:middle
to move the-they're going to get

39:28.546 --> 39:30.636 align:middle
that rate right back
up to where it was.

39:31.106 --> 39:34.206 align:middle
And we, we assume
they'll do that over time.

39:34.836 --> 39:36.456 align:middle
So that's how we think about it.

39:36.456 --> 39:37.056 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

39:37.056 --> 39:37.286 align:middle
Thank you.

39:37.286 --> 39:38.166 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:38.726 --> 39:39.846 align:middle
Chris Rugaber.

39:40.336 --> 39:40.646 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

39:41.816 --> 39:42.886 align:middle
Hi, thank you.

39:43.156 --> 39:45.086 align:middle
Chris Rugaber, Associated Press.

39:45.166 --> 39:47.446 align:middle
I wanted-you talked
about the shortcomings

39:47.446 --> 39:49.876 align:middle
of the SEP in this environment.

39:49.876 --> 39:53.726 align:middle
I wanted to ask about or
throw in an additional thing,

39:53.726 --> 39:56.516 align:middle
which is the fact that it's
happening before transition.

39:57.276 --> 39:59.066 align:middle
Is there still some
value perhaps

39:59.066 --> 40:02.816 align:middle
in the public knowing what other
Fed officials are thinking-the

40:02.816 --> 40:05.556 align:middle
ones who will stay on for the
rest of the year and beyond?

40:05.556 --> 40:09.066 align:middle
And does that even handicap
your successor in any way?

40:10.396 --> 40:14.936 align:middle
Well, just in terms of having
a whole Committee expressing

40:14.936 --> 40:15.516 align:middle
its views.

40:16.676 --> 40:18.436 align:middle
Is that something
that locks them

40:18.436 --> 40:19.226 align:middle
in for the rest of the year?

40:19.586 --> 40:19.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:20.016 --> 40:20.556 align:middle
No. No, no.

40:20.556 --> 40:24.456 align:middle
Never. The SEP is
never-people are more than happy

40:24.456 --> 40:26.666 align:middle
to change their SEP dots.

40:26.666 --> 40:28.336 align:middle
They're in no way bound by them.

40:28.446 --> 40:33.956 align:middle
It's your opinion at a moment
in time, which can change based

40:33.956 --> 40:35.736 align:middle
on events, sometimes
very quickly.

40:37.046 --> 40:39.556 align:middle
So it doesn't-it
never locks people in.

40:39.846 --> 40:40.966 align:middle
People are more than happy

40:40.966 --> 40:43.546 align:middle
to be proven wrong,
in either direction.

40:43.716 --> 40:46.156 align:middle
And so I just think we do it.

40:46.496 --> 40:48.626 align:middle
And we-this was-I
mentioned, this was a time

40:48.626 --> 40:49.676 align:middle
when it's very hard to do it.

40:49.676 --> 40:52.576 align:middle
March 18, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 22

40:52.576 --> 40:53.946 align:middle
of 25 During the pandemic,

40:53.946 --> 40:54.876 align:middle
we actually took one
meeting off, I think.

40:54.876 --> 40:57.326 align:middle
But we don't like to do that,
just because it's hard to do.

40:57.326 --> 40:58.546 align:middle
We should just keep doing it.

40:58.986 --> 41:02.446 align:middle
But I just would say for this
one, though, I think it's,

41:02.656 --> 41:06.576 align:middle
it's more than-even more than
usual good to take the forecast

41:06.576 --> 41:08.066 align:middle
with a grain of salt
because subject

41:08.096 --> 41:10.556 align:middle
to just very high
levels of uncertainty.

41:10.556 --> 41:11.616 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:11.616 --> 41:14.556 align:middle
Brian. BRIAN CHEUNG.

41:15.616 --> 41:16.266 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

41:16.266 --> 41:17.636 align:middle
Brian Cheung with NBC News.

41:17.696 --> 41:20.186 align:middle
So you mentioned at the
beginning of this conversation

41:20.186 --> 41:21.656 align:middle
that you're well
aware of the history

41:21.656 --> 41:23.366 align:middle
of inflation running
above target.

41:23.886 --> 41:26.186 align:middle
It seems like that might
be what's underpinning the

41:26.226 --> 41:27.936 align:middle
affordability problem
that we're hearing a lot

41:27.936 --> 41:29.286 align:middle
about on Main Street.

41:29.286 --> 41:32.866 align:middle
Are there any signs to you that
that's impacting the psychology

41:32.866 --> 41:36.336 align:middle
of the consumer, especially
given that they're a big driver

41:36.336 --> 41:37.576 align:middle
of overall economic activity?

41:37.916 --> 41:38.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:38.766 --> 41:42.076 align:middle
I, you know, I'm not sure
what's, what that's all about.

41:42.076 --> 41:44.976 align:middle
But I'll tell you what I think
and what we see in surveys,

41:44.976 --> 41:46.336 align:middle
and that is, you know,

41:46.596 --> 41:51.086 align:middle
people-there were big price
increases all around the world,

41:51.086 --> 41:53.666 align:middle
by the way, everywhere
in countries

41:53.666 --> 41:55.736 align:middle
like the United States had
basically the same kind

41:55.776 --> 41:55.956 align:middle
of thing.

41:55.956 --> 41:58.306 align:middle
It was global inflation
coming out of the pandemic.

41:58.986 --> 42:00.446 align:middle
And everywhere people
are feeling

42:00.446 --> 42:03.206 align:middle
like they-real wages
have been going

42:03.206 --> 42:05.606 align:middle
up in the United States now
for three years, roughly,

42:06.136 --> 42:09.386 align:middle
but people are not feeling
like-they're not feeling good

42:09.386 --> 42:10.016 align:middle
about it yet.

42:10.016 --> 42:14.006 align:middle
And it will take some years
of positive real earning gains

42:14.436 --> 42:16.096 align:middle
for people to feel
good again, we think.

42:16.666 --> 42:19.656 align:middle
But you're right-when
you talk to people,

42:19.846 --> 42:21.256 align:middle
they do feel squeezed.

42:21.646 --> 42:25.906 align:middle
You know, there are areas where,
where prices are still going up,

42:25.906 --> 42:27.476 align:middle
like insurance-various
different kinds

42:27.476 --> 42:30.266 align:middle
of insurance are getting
more and more expensive,

42:30.266 --> 42:32.316 align:middle
and that's just catching
up, really,

42:32.316 --> 42:35.646 align:middle
from inflationary pressures
that take a while to get

42:35.646 --> 42:37.116 align:middle
to the price-into the price.

42:37.616 --> 42:40.956 align:middle
So we take it very seriously,
we don't dismiss it at all.

42:41.146 --> 42:43.056 align:middle
It's a very real thing
from the standpoint

42:43.056 --> 42:45.756 align:middle
of what-of what people
are March 18,

42:45.756 --> 42:48.706 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 23

42:48.706 --> 42:49.466 align:middle
of 25 experiencing.

42:49.496 --> 42:50.486 align:middle
And what it does
for us is, you know,

42:50.486 --> 42:52.266 align:middle
it makes us even more
committed, if that's possible,

42:52.306 --> 42:55.686 align:middle
to getting inflation back to 2
percent on a sustained basis.

42:55.776 --> 42:56.056 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

42:56.056 --> 42:58.456 align:middle
How does the Fed's independence
play into the ability

42:58.456 --> 42:59.546 align:middle
to address affordability?

42:59.986 --> 43:00.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:00.816 --> 43:03.476 align:middle
Well, independence is what
allows us to do our jobs,

43:04.176 --> 43:06.816 align:middle
and stable prices is half
of our mandate-it's one

43:06.816 --> 43:10.786 align:middle
of our two mandates, maximum
employment being the other.

43:10.786 --> 43:14.436 align:middle
I mean, look at every advanced
economy that looks anything

43:14.436 --> 43:16.676 align:middle
like the United States
anywhere in the world

43:16.676 --> 43:19.006 align:middle
in a market economy,
in a democracy,

43:19.006 --> 43:24.796 align:middle
and you'll see pretty much
Fed-central bank independence

43:24.796 --> 43:27.076 align:middle
that looks a lot like, in
some cases stronger than,

43:27.076 --> 43:27.606 align:middle
what we have.

43:27.706 --> 43:30.116 align:middle
But it's critical
that we have that so

43:30.116 --> 43:32.876 align:middle
that we can do the things
when we need to do them

43:33.006 --> 43:34.586 align:middle
to preserve price stability.

43:35.246 --> 43:38.336 align:middle
And it just-it's an
accepted standard practice

43:38.336 --> 43:41.286 align:middle
and I think has a lot of
support, certainly in Congress,

43:41.286 --> 43:45.556 align:middle
which is where our oversight
is-is in Congress-and you see

43:45.556 --> 43:47.306 align:middle
that among Democrats
and Republicans

43:47.306 --> 43:48.286 align:middle
in the House and the Senate.

43:48.286 --> 43:50.466 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:50.466 --> 43:50.926 align:middle
Richard Escobedo.

43:52.076 --> 43:52.516 align:middle
RICHARD ESCOBEDO.

43:54.456 --> 43:55.066 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

43:55.066 --> 43:56.416 align:middle
Richard Escobedo at CBS News.

43:56.766 --> 43:59.156 align:middle
Thanks for doing this.

43:59.156 --> 43:59.776 align:middle
Two questions.

43:59.776 --> 44:03.026 align:middle
One, how high would oil prices
and broader inflation have

44:03.026 --> 44:05.846 align:middle
to go, and for how
long, for the Committee

44:05.846 --> 44:07.266 align:middle
to consider hiking rates?

44:08.476 --> 44:09.536 align:middle
And then, secondly,

44:09.756 --> 44:12.056 align:middle
diesel prices are rising
faster than gas prices.

44:12.456 --> 44:15.576 align:middle
How concerned are you that the
increase in diesel might drive

44:15.576 --> 44:19.376 align:middle
up food and goods prices and
lead to broader inflation?

44:19.856 --> 44:20.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:20.116 --> 44:23.936 align:middle
Yeah, I'm not going to give you
an example or specific answer

44:23.936 --> 44:24.936 align:middle
on the first question.

44:24.936 --> 44:27.936 align:middle
We're prepared to do what needs
to be done, but I wouldn't want

44:28.216 --> 44:30.586 align:middle
to hypothesize about what
that-what that might be.

44:31.116 --> 44:33.306 align:middle
In terms of diesel prices,
so your question there is,

44:33.306 --> 44:34.836 align:middle
are we concerned about it?

44:34.836 --> 44:37.236 align:middle
I mean- March 18, 2026 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

44:37.236 --> 44:38.406 align:middle
Page 24 of 25 RICHARD ESCOBEDO.

44:38.406 --> 44:41.196 align:middle
How concerned, and whether or
not it will drive up, you know,

44:41.386 --> 44:43.696 align:middle
the cost of food and other
goods that really rely on it

44:43.696 --> 44:44.626 align:middle
to get across the country?

44:44.626 --> 44:44.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:44.866 --> 44:47.206 align:middle
I think it's a more
general concern than that.

44:47.206 --> 44:50.386 align:middle
It's diesel prices because
that's, that's transportation

44:50.436 --> 44:51.616 align:middle
of food and things like that.

44:51.906 --> 44:55.706 align:middle
There are just lots of ways that
oil and derivatives of oil get

44:55.706 --> 44:57.076 align:middle
into the production

44:57.076 --> 44:59.916 align:middle
and transportation
of many, many things.

44:59.916 --> 45:03.466 align:middle
And those-they're big
effects in headline inflation,

45:03.466 --> 45:06.196 align:middle
but things like that
leak into core as well.

45:07.006 --> 45:09.126 align:middle
The effects may not be as big,

45:09.126 --> 45:10.956 align:middle
but they're quite real
and they're material.

45:11.386 --> 45:13.176 align:middle
So we are aware of that,
and we're watching it,

45:13.176 --> 45:14.866 align:middle
but we're right at
the beginning of this,

45:14.866 --> 45:17.756 align:middle
and we don't know how big-you
just don't know how big this

45:17.756 --> 45:19.216 align:middle
will be, and how long it lasts.

45:19.386 --> 45:21.636 align:middle
It may or may not be something

45:21.636 --> 45:24.426 align:middle
that really makes a big
imprint on the U.S. economy.

45:24.426 --> 45:25.556 align:middle
We're just going to
have to wait and see.

45:26.516 --> 45:27.076 align:middle
RICHARD ESCOBEDO.

45:28.676 --> 45:29.036 align:middle
Thank you.

45:29.036 --> 45:29.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:29.606 --> 45:30.906 align:middle
Archie, for the last question.

45:31.426 --> 45:32.146 align:middle
ARCHIE HALL.

45:32.146 --> 45:32.866 align:middle
Thanks very much.

45:33.156 --> 45:35.806 align:middle
Just to sort of stare into
the future for a little bit,

45:35.806 --> 45:38.746 align:middle
I was looking at the
longer-run bits of the SEP.

45:38.746 --> 45:41.966 align:middle
I noticed that the growth
estimate had been revised

45:41.966 --> 45:45.076 align:middle
up a good chunk from 1.8
to 2 [percent] and the kind

45:45.076 --> 45:46.746 align:middle
of long-term fed
funds rate as well.

45:46.746 --> 45:48.796 align:middle
It would be great just to hear,
is that-is that AI productivity,

45:48.796 --> 45:50.106 align:middle
or what's going on
there exactly?

45:50.306 --> 45:50.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:50.596 --> 45:53.096 align:middle
Yeah, well, I think it's,
it's just productivity.

45:53.096 --> 45:56.766 align:middle
We actually started seeing
meaningfully higher productivity

45:56.766 --> 45:59.166 align:middle
some years ago-four years
ago, five years ago.

45:59.756 --> 46:03.506 align:middle
And that's not because
of generative AI,

46:03.506 --> 46:05.626 align:middle
it's-it may-we'll-we won't know

46:05.626 --> 46:07.406 align:middle
for years what it's
really due to.

46:07.936 --> 46:09.536 align:middle
But it could be due
to the kind of things

46:09.536 --> 46:12.656 align:middle
that people did during
the pandemic to economize

46:12.656 --> 46:14.856 align:middle
and somehow become
more productive,

46:14.856 --> 46:17.136 align:middle
because there was an
incredible labor shortage.

46:17.736 --> 46:24.016 align:middle
I think economic forecasters
are very skeptical of,

46:24.866 --> 46:29.686 align:middle
of periods of high productivity,
because they're so rare,

46:30.096 --> 46:31.396 align:middle
and they're often revised away.

46:31.396 --> 46:32.316 align:middle
And for many-I never
thought I'd see March 18,

46:32.316 --> 46:34.496 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 25

46:34.496 --> 46:36.756 align:middle
of 25 this many years of
really high productivity and,

46:36.756 --> 46:38.176 align:middle
by the way, expect
it to continue.

46:38.916 --> 46:40.016 align:middle
And we haven't really started

46:40.016 --> 46:43.086 align:middle
to see the effects
of, of generative AI.

46:43.626 --> 46:45.696 align:middle
And that should-that should
certainly contribute to that.

46:45.696 --> 46:48.016 align:middle
So it's quite unusual,

46:48.016 --> 46:51.396 align:middle
and it's-this is the-higher
productivity is the thing

46:51.396 --> 46:53.466 align:middle
that allows incomes
to rise over time.

46:54.116 --> 46:54.926 align:middle
And so it's a great thing.

46:54.926 --> 46:55.706 align:middle
Was that your question?

46:55.946 --> 46:56.186 align:middle
ARCHIE HALL.

46:56.186 --> 46:57.866 align:middle
Yeah, no, I was just
curious what lay behind that.

46:57.866 --> 47:01.266 align:middle
And just briefly on that
as well to finish up,

47:02.426 --> 47:03.846 align:middle
you hear the argument
a lot, this is sort

47:03.846 --> 47:05.476 align:middle
of a disinflationary
story, and if you believe

47:05.476 --> 47:07.776 align:middle
that productivity thing, that
should also weigh down inflation

47:07.776 --> 47:10.766 align:middle
over time, maybe then kind of
pull down where rates end up.

47:10.766 --> 47:12.666 align:middle
Do you kind of agree with
that line of thinking,

47:12.666 --> 47:14.546 align:middle
or do you think that's
not how you should think

47:14.546 --> 47:15.746 align:middle
about whether rates-
CHAIR POWELL.

47:15.746 --> 47:17.966 align:middle
I think you have to be cautious
about that, in particular

47:17.966 --> 47:20.376 align:middle
if you're talking about
AI, about generative AI.

47:20.376 --> 47:23.986 align:middle
So, remember, in the short
term what's happening is we're

47:24.106 --> 47:25.726 align:middle
building data centers
everywhere.

47:25.726 --> 47:30.556 align:middle
And that's actually putting
pressure on all kinds of goods

47:30.556 --> 47:32.876 align:middle
and services that go into
building these things.

47:32.876 --> 47:35.036 align:middle
So that's actually
probably pushing inflation

47:35.036 --> 47:35.796 align:middle
up at the margin.

47:35.796 --> 47:42.396 align:middle
In addition, it probably
raises the neutral rate in,

47:42.396 --> 47:43.456 align:middle
in the near term.

47:43.456 --> 47:45.436 align:middle
You're looking at-you're
not looking at something

47:45.436 --> 47:47.406 align:middle
that would immediately
call for lower rates

47:47.506 --> 47:49.446 align:middle
or that would be
lowering inflation.

47:49.836 --> 47:51.386 align:middle
Over time, though, sure,

47:51.386 --> 47:55.796 align:middle
it's a-it can be a-if it's
expanding potential output,

47:55.796 --> 47:57.816 align:middle
which is what
productivity-higher productivity

47:57.816 --> 47:59.596 align:middle
does, then it actually can be.

47:59.866 --> 48:03.366 align:middle
But I think it's an empirical
question because it's really,

48:03.366 --> 48:06.376 align:middle
is demand growing faster or
slower than the supply side?

48:06.996 --> 48:11.726 align:middle
And I think we just don't know
that answer ex ante going in.

48:11.726 --> 48:13.596 align:middle
We'll just have to wait and see.

48:13.826 --> 48:15.006 align:middle
Thank you very much.

